WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.840
90% of companies are ramping AI
investments, with hyperscale capex

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.800
hitting $405 billion.

3
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.720
Yet only 1% consider themselves AI mature.

4
00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.640
Every boardroom has a slide deck outlining
an AI strategy,

5
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.160
but nearly 90% of these companies are
completely stuck in

6
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.000
neutral. They're trapped in what's known
as pilot purgatory.

7
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.680
This is a state where isolated experiments
succeed, but they

8
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.960
never actually graduate to
enterprise-scale production.

9
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.080
When you see failure at this massive
scale, it's not

10
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.920
the technology malfunctioning.

11
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.120
It's a structural flaw in how these
businesses measure their

12
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.440
own reality. Think about how a typical AI
initiative unfolds.

13
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.600
A team launches a pilot project.

14
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.320
The code is clean, the outputs are fast,
and on

15
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:51.280
a technical level, it works exactly as
designed.

16
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.680
But when it comes time to scale this
working tool

17
00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:56.880
across the company, leadership looks at
the numbers, cuts the

18
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.360
funding, and lets the project quietly die.

19
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:03.400
The standard corporate reaction is to
blame the algorithm, fire

20
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.960
the vendor, or complain about the data.

21
00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.760
But in this specific case, all of those
elements were

22
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:13.720
working perfectly. Reverse engineering why
a successful tool gets abandoned

23
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.080
points directly to a fatal error in how
companies calculate

24
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.920
their return on investment.

25
00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.480
In 1987, the economist Robert Solow
noticed something strange.

26
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.920
The computer age was everywhere, except in
the national productivity

27
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.200
statistics. The numbers were entirely
flat.

28
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.960
We are watching that exact paradox repeat
itself right now.

29
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:39.400
We have extraordinary, working technical
capabilities, coexisting with stubborn, stagnant

30
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.320
productivity numbers. This isn't a glitch
unique to computing or

31
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.600
AI. It is a historical pattern that
applies to every

32
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.640
general-purpose technology. Whether an
economy is adopting steam power, mechanization,

33
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:54.840
or electricity, the initial rollout always
looks like a statistical

34
00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.880
failure. Economists Eric Brinielsson,
Daniel Rock, and Chad Cyberson developed

35
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.840
a framework that finally makes sense of
this mystery.

36
00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.400
They call it the productivity J-curve.

37
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.560
It proves that when organizations adopt a
transformative technology, their

38
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.040
measured productivity actually drops
before generating any major gains.

39
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.480
The reason for this drop comes down to
intangible capital.

40
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.000
These are the invisible, secondary
investments required to make a

41
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:24.240
general-purpose technology function.

42
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.200
During the early adoption phase, real,
measurable resources, money, time,

43
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.200
and attention, are being spent.

44
00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.080
As a result, measured output drops
sharply.

45
00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:37.200
Down in this trough, companies are
spending heavily to build

46
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.720
crucial intangible assets, redesigned
business processes, clean data governance, and

47
00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.040
comprehensive workforce reskilling.

48
00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.600
Because these assets don't show up on a
standard balance

49
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.000
sheet, corporate dashboards look terrible.

50
00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:53.480
They falsely report that the company is
actively losing ground.

51
00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.480
But this painful valley of reduced
performance is actually the

52
00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.560
critical accumulation phase.

53
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.400
Without these hidden assets, future growth
is impossible.

54
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.120
This brings us back to the autopsy of that
perfect

55
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.200
AI pilot we looked at earlier.

56
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.440
That pilot didn't fail technically.

57
00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.720
It failed because it was evaluated at the
absolute lowest

58
00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:15.640
point of the J-curve trough.

59
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.800
Leadership looked at a multi-year
infrastructure investment and judged it

60
00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:21.880
using a quarterly returns model.

61
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.320
The numbers didn't justify scaling, so
they killed it.

62
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:29.160
If you evaluate a generational shift in
technology using a

63
00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:33.560
quarterly measurement framework, your
innovation will perpetually stall.

64
00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:38.600
That tiny 1% fraction of AI mature
organizations succeed because

65
00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:41.560
they use an entirely different accounting
model.

66
00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:45.840
Research from Boston Consulting Group
quantifies exactly how these successful

67
00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:50.800
companies allocate their resources using a
70-2010 heuristic.

68
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.560
This chart shows the breakdown.

69
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.160
10% of the effort goes to the algorithms,
20% goes

70
00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:00.320
to data and infrastructure, and a massive
70% goes entirely

71
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.560
toward people and process redesign.

72
00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:06.080
Most failing enterprises have this ratio
completely inverted.

73
00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.040
They spend the vast majority of their
budget procuring software

74
00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.640
and ignore everything else.

75
00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:11.840
The math is clear.

76
00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:14.800
For every dollar spent on a new model, $7
must

77
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:18.000
be spent rewiring the humans and the
organization around it.

78
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.280
To survive the troth, leadership has to
abandon the strategy

79
00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:24.480
of funding dozens of disconnected
bottom-up experiments.

80
00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.040
Instead, companies need to fund
comprehensive end-to-end process transformations, run

81
00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.000
by cross-functional teams with multi-year
horizons.

82
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.280
If integrating AI causes short-term
friction and drops your measured

83
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.040
productivity, it isn't failing.

84
00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:39.760
That valley of reduced performance is
simply the mandatory cost

85
00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.280
of entry to build the future.

